From: Gemini PDA Linux port <mratan/gemini-linux>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: mediatek: mt6797: number the imgsys clock IDs from 0

The MT6797 imgsys clock IDs ran 1..4 with CLK_IMG_NR 5, while
clk-mt6797-img.c sizes its provider with ARRAY_SIZE(img_clks) = 4. So the
allocation covers indices 0..3 and CLK_IMG_LARB6 (4) falls outside it --
any consumer asking for it gets -ENOENT and the clock can never be handed
out.

Raising num_clks to CLK_IMG_NR is NOT the fix, and it is worth writing
down why: mtk_clk_desc uses num_clks for BOTH the clk_data allocation and
the iteration over clks[] in __mtk_clk_simple_probe(). A 5 makes the gate
registration read img_clks[4], off the end of the array. Tried on build
#86: LK runs, the panel lights, then the kernel dies with no console and
no USB gadget, and recovery took SP Flash Tool over the preloader window.

Numbering the IDs from 0 fixes it cleanly. It is safe here because
nothing else in tree consumes an MT6797 imgsys clock -- this SoC's first
consumer is the camera bridge added alongside.

The effect is not cosmetic. With the clocks reachable and enabled in the
vendor's order (IMG before CAM, as ISP_EnableClock() does), the pass1 ISP
becomes accessible for the first time:

  before   CAM_A 0 non-zero of 256, CAM_B 0 of 256
  after    CAM_A 51 non-zero of 256, CAM_B 51 of 256

and every static CAM_B register matches the value read off the vendor
while its camera streamed -- +0x00c 40000000, +0x014 00000010,
+0x040 00000002, +0x070 0000f000, +0x078 20140831, +0x07c 65990000,
+0x0a8 7fffffff, +0x0ac 000007bf.

That retires the last hypothesis on #66. CAM_A was never bus-protected;
it was unclocked, because the clock it needed could not be requested.
---
--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-img.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-img.c
@@ -28,6 +28,23 @@
 
 static const struct mtk_clk_desc img_desc = {
 	.clks = img_clks,
+	/*
+	 * DO NOT "fix" this to CLK_IMG_NR. It looks wrong -- the IDs run 1..4
+	 * so CLK_IMG_LARB6 (4) is outside the 0..3 that ARRAY_SIZE gives, and
+	 * consumers asking for it get -ENOENT -- but num_clks is used for BOTH
+	 * the clk_data allocation AND the iteration over clks[] in
+	 * __mtk_clk_simple_probe(). Raising it to 5 makes the gate
+	 * registration read img_clks[4], off the end of the array, and the
+	 * kernel does not survive it. Tried on build #86: boots to LK, panel
+	 * comes up, then dies with no console and no USB gadget; recovery
+	 * needed SP Flash Tool over the preloader.
+	 *
+	 * The underlying bug is real and still unfixed: this provider cannot
+	 * hand out CLK_IMG_LARB6. Fixing it properly means either renumbering
+	 * the IDs in dt-bindings/clock/mt6797-clk.h to start at 0 (safe here --
+	 * nothing else consumes them) or teaching mtk_clk_desc to size the
+	 * allocation separately from the array length.
+	 */
 	.num_clks = ARRAY_SIZE(img_clks),
 };
 
--- a/include/dt-bindings/clock/mt6797-clk.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/mt6797-clk.h
@@ -236,11 +236,24 @@
 #define	CLK_CAM_NR				7
 
 /* IMG_SYS */
-#define	CLK_IMG_FDVT				1
-#define	CLK_IMG_DPE				2
-#define	CLK_IMG_DIP				3
-#define	CLK_IMG_LARB6				4
-#define	CLK_IMG_NR				5
+/*
+ * These IDs start at 0 on purpose. They used to run 1..4 with CLK_IMG_NR 5,
+ * and clk-mt6797-img.c sizes its provider with ARRAY_SIZE(img_clks) = 4 --
+ * so CLK_IMG_LARB6 fell outside the allocated 0..3 and consumers got -ENOENT.
+ *
+ * Raising num_clks to CLK_IMG_NR is NOT the fix: mtk_clk_desc uses num_clks
+ * for both the allocation and the iteration over clks[], so a 5 reads
+ * img_clks[4] off the end of the array and the kernel dies at boot (build
+ * #86, 2026-08-25 -- recovery needed SP Flash Tool over the preloader).
+ *
+ * Renumbering is safe here because nothing else in tree consumes an MT6797
+ * imgsys clock; this SoC's first consumer is the camera bridge.
+ */
+#define	CLK_IMG_FDVT				0
+#define	CLK_IMG_DPE				1
+#define	CLK_IMG_DIP				2
+#define	CLK_IMG_LARB6				3
+#define	CLK_IMG_NR				4
 
 /* MM_SYS */
 #define	CLK_MM_SMI_COMMON			1
